Yes. There’s a scientifically defensible version of that idea, provided we separate epigenetic regulation from the broader—and probably more powerful—phenomenon of phenotypic plasticity.

Comparative morphology gives you the first clue: humans are not blank biological machines. We inherited a mammalian/primate body whose architecture presupposes certain classes of input—locomotion, load, thermal variation, sunlight/darkness, social hierarchy and cooperation, exploration, threat, play, scarcity/abundance cycles, tactile contact, complex terrain, etc. Our morphology is effectively a fossilized record of recurring environmental problems.

So I’d formulate your rewilding principle roughly as:

Reintroduce ecologically meaningful inputs to reveal, maintain, or amplify latent capacities already supported by the organism’s architecture.

What makes this interesting is that the effect occurs across several layers simultaneously:

environmental signal → sensory perception → nervous/endocrine response → gene-expression changes → tissue remodeling → altered capability → changed behavior → changed future exposure

Epigenetics participates in that chain—DNA methylation, histone modification, chromatin accessibility and related mechanisms can change gene expression in response to experience—but it isn’t a biological character-selection menu. You generally can’t decide, “turn on the courage methylation pattern.” Neural plasticity, endocrine adaptation, muscle/tendon/bone remodeling, immune conditioning and ordinary learning are often doing at least as much work.

But your larger pattern survives.

Take aggression. Instead of treating it as one indivisible trait, comparative biology decomposes it into things such as threat detection, territoriality, willingness to approach, pain tolerance, sympathetic activation, dominance behavior and inhibition. Different environmental regimes tune those components differently.

Now perform the same decomposition on other traits.

Endurance can be pushed with sustained locomotion, metabolic demand and recovery. Mitochondrial density, capillarization, cardiac output and movement economy adapt.

Cold tolerance can be altered through repeated cold exposure: vascular responses, thermal perception and possibly brown-adipose thermogenesis adapt.

Physical robustness comes from loading in multiple vectors rather than only perfectly standardized gym movements. Bone, connective tissue, proprioception and motor control remodel according to the stresses repeatedly imposed on them.

Spatial intelligence can be trained by navigation through irregular environments rather than perpetual GPS-following. That is overwhelmingly neural plasticity rather than some magical epigenetic switch, but it’s the same ecological-input principle.

Vigilance can be increased by uncertainty and threat exposure—but interestingly, you can overshoot. Chronic threat can create pathological hypervigilance. So the adaptive target is usually not maximal activation but controllable activation.

And this is where your idea becomes more useful than merely “make humans tougher.”

You could selectively rewild gentleness.

Mammalian biology contains attachment, play, grooming, caregiving and coalition-building systems just as surely as it contains aggression. Repeated caregiving, cooperative tasks, physical affection, shared meals and play reinforce those circuits.

You can rewild curiosity through novelty-rich environments.

You can rewild patience through activities with unavoidable natural latency—gardening, fishing, hunting, woodworking, navigation, animal care.

You can rewild coordination through climbing, throwing, balancing, carrying, crawling and irregular terrain.

You can rewild sensory resolution by repeatedly making smell, sound, touch, peripheral vision or visual discrimination relevant to successful action.

You can even rewild something like agency by designing environments in which perception reliably leads to consequential action: I noticed something → I acted → the environment changed → I learned.

That feedback loop is profoundly different from environments where nearly every stimulus is informational and almost none require embodied response.

There’s also a useful distinction between rewilding and training.

Training often says:

desired output → engineer an exercise producing it.

Rewilding says:

desired capability → reconstruct the class of environment in which that capability naturally becomes advantageous.

That second method sometimes produces adaptations you didn’t know enough to train directly. You are specifying the selection pressure rather than every internal mechanism.

And comparative morphology gives you a way of choosing those pressures. Ask:

What is this structure apparently designed to do? What inputs would historically have made that structure valuable? What happens when those inputs disappear? What happens when they are restored in a controlled dose?

That becomes a fairly powerful experimental framework.

The deepest version of your idea is therefore not really:

“Activate our primitive genes.”

It is:

“Use ancestral and ecologically coherent constraints as developmental operators.”

Then you don’t have to limit it to strength, aggression, survival or stereotypically “wild” characteristics. You can choose the phenotype-space you want to explore: resilience, cooperation, dexterity, attention, exploration, attachment, endurance, creativity, calm-under-load, sensory acuity.

And there is one especially important wrinkle: wild animals aren’t maximized for traits; they’re calibrated. A permanently aggressive wolf would be a terrible wolf. An animal that never sleeps because it is maximally vigilant dies. Evolution tends to favor regulation—knowing when to deploy a capability and when to suppress it.

So the really sophisticated form of human rewilding would aim not merely at more trait, but at greater controllable range:

calm ↔ aggression
rest ↔ exertion
solitude ↔ affiliation
focus ↔ exploration
scarcity competence ↔ abundance competence
softness ↔ hardness

That looks less like returning to some imagined primitive human and more like recovering a wider adaptive repertoire.

And that is probably where comparative morphology, developmental plasticity, behavioral ecology and your structural-function principle genuinely intersect.